**Goal.** The plan's own work order (§9) is 14 items; 1–10 are marked "bezinwazyjne" and 11–14 touch live services. Items 1–10 are all present and wired end to end: port-occupancy check naming the culprit (`ZajetoscPortow`, new PORT button), `ICaddyProcessController` + preflight, `CaddyRoute`/`ICaddyfileWriter` with per-TLS-mode block splitting, `CaddyInstaller` (Settings.json source per D11, SHA-256, WinSW, `sc create start= auto`, parameterised `sc failure`), the WinSW-hosted watchdog plus main-window status, `ICertificateProvider` (Windows store → PEM chain + `tls internal`), transactional write + `Reconcile()`, the inverted TLS rule in the qualifier with per-family report and the §3.1 "binding unknown" warning, the three new setting types with SPA `try_files`, and `OpenPorts` extended with `SERVICEPORT_HTTPS`. Items 11–14 (family A/B/C cutover, ACME) are deliberately not shipped and are held behind `BramkiWydaniowe.PrzejeciePortuUslugiGotowe = false`, documented as such — so today only websites can pick Caddy. That is a faithful reading of a staged plan, so I count the goal as met. Two sub-items of the plan are absent without being named as deferred: the comparison harness in §9 item 10, and the `#caddy-acme` list position that §5.5's table promises (the code ties it to step 12).

